Close the open(2) existence oracles -- the write-intent one and the O_CREAT one.

  open(p, O_WRONLY)              -> EROFS   where an absent name gives ENOENT
  open(p, O_RDWR)                -> EROFS   ""
  open(p, O_WRONLY|O_TRUNC)      -> EROFS   ""
  open(p, O_CREAT|O_EXCL, 0600)  -> EEXIST  where an absent name gives EROFS
  open(p, O_CREAT|O_RDONLY,0600) -> ENOENT  where an absent name gives EROFS

TWO MECHANISMS, ONE FUNCTION

The write-intent forms are the same sb_permission() short-circuit
ghost_access.patch documents: inode_permission() answers -EROFS on a read-only
superblock before do_inode_permission() dispatches to the engine's hijacked
->permission. do_open()'s own O_TRUNC mnt_want_write() answers it earlier still.

The O_CREAT forms are the ordering ghost_create.patch found in filename_create()
-- existence decided ahead of the write check -- reached by the ONE create path
that does not route through filename_create(). path_openat() runs
link_path_walk() and open_last_lookups() itself, and do_open() then returns

	if (open_flag & O_CREAT) {
		if ((open_flag & O_EXCL) && !(file->f_mode & FMODE_CREATED))
			return -EEXIST;		/* ahead of may_open() */

for a positive dentry, while an absent name took got_write == false in
open_last_lookups(), so lookup_open() set create_error = -EROFS, cleared O_CREAT
and reported that instead. Without O_EXCL the split is the same one inverted:
the hidden path reaches may_open() -> inode_permission() with no MAY_WRITE (so
sb_permission() passes) and the engine answers -ENOENT, where the absent name
still answers -EROFS. Verified against v6.12 fs/namei.c.

WHY THE ANSWER IS NOT UNIFORMLY -ENOENT

Every other guard in this directory returns -ENOENT because that is what an
absent path reports for that syscall. Here it depends on O_CREAT: without it the
walk would have failed with -ENOENT, with it the absent name would have been
refused a create with mnt_want_write()'s own error. The guard therefore probes
mnt_want_write() for the O_CREAT case and reports exactly what it says.

On a WRITABLE mount an absent name would be CREATED here, and that cannot be
emulated without clobbering the file being hidden -- so the stock answer stands,
no worse than an unpatched kernel. That is ghost_create.patch's `err2 &&`
reasoning, in the one place it has to be spelled with a live probe because
do_open() has no err2 to consult.

WHY WRITE INTENT ONLY

open(2) is the hottest syscall this directory touches. A plain read-only open of
a hidden path already answers -ENOENT through the engine's ->permission, so the
predicate would be evaluated on a large fraction of all opens to confirm an
answer that is already correct. The gate is `(open_flag & (O_CREAT | O_TRUNC)) ||
(op->acc_mode & MAY_WRITE)`, which is exactly the leaking set. FMODE_CREATED is
excluded because a file this very open created can never be a pre-existing
hidden path.

WHERE THE GUARD GOES, AND WHY THERE

Immediately after do_open()'s complete_walk() block -- the earliest point at
which nd->path is legitimised ref-walk -- and before audit_inode(), the O_CREAT
tests and the O_TRUNC write reference. An absent name is never audited and never
reaches any of the three.

This is the second _ghost hunk in do_open(); ghost_notdir.patch places the other,
about fifteen lines below, on the LOOKUP_DIRECTORY test. They do not overlap and
apply in either order. The overlap in EFFECT is deliberate: with both applied the
guard here answers open(p, O_DIRECTORY) first, and the notdir hunk stays as the
narrower, independently-verified backstop.

TWO LINES OF CONTEXT, NOT THREE, AND WHY

The line below `audit_inode(nd->name, nd->path.dentry, 0);` is the one thing that
differs across the fleet -- 5.10 has `if (open_flag & O_CREAT) {`, 5.15/6.1 have
`mnt_userns = mnt_user_ns(nd->path.mnt);`, 6.6/6.12 have
`idmap = mnt_idmap(nd->path.mnt);`. Two lines of context on each side is the most
that is common to all five, and it is SYMMETRIC, which is what `patch -F0`
actually cares about (see ghost_create.patch's note: an asymmetric hunk was
refused on every tree while -U3 and -U4 both applied). The five-line pre-image is
unique in fs/namei.c on every one of the five trees -- checked, not assumed -- so
there is nowhere else for it to land.

APPLIES TO

One file, 5.10 through 6.12. Dry-run at -F0 against the pinned OnePlus trees for
all five: applies to every one, offsets only, and the guard lands inside
do_open() on each. Generated by editing the real file and running `diff -u2 -p`,
never by hand.

--- a/fs/namei.c
+++ b/fs/namei.c
@@ -3742,4 +3742,50 @@ static int do_open(struct nameidata *nd,
 			return error;
 	}
+	{
+		extern bool ghost_hidden_path(const struct path *gp);
+
+		/*
+		 * Two families of open(2) are answered here without the engine's
+		 * hijacked ->permission ever being consulted:
+		 *
+		 *  - WRITE intent (O_WRONLY / O_RDWR / O_TRUNC). sb_permission(),
+		 *    inside inode_permission(), answers -EROFS on a read-only ROM
+		 *    mount before do_inode_permission() dispatches; and do_open()'s
+		 *    own O_TRUNC mnt_want_write() below answers it earlier still.
+		 *    An absent name gives -ENOENT out of the walk.
+		 *
+		 *  - O_CREAT. The O_EXCL test a few lines below returns -EEXIST
+		 *    ahead of may_open(), while an absent name on a read-only
+		 *    mount gets -EROFS out of lookup_open()'s create_error. Same
+		 *    ordering ghost_create.patch documents for filename_create() --
+		 *    on the one create path that does NOT route through it.
+		 *
+		 * The faithful answer therefore differs by case: -ENOENT where the
+		 * lookup itself would have failed, and mnt_want_write()'s own error
+		 * where an absent name would have been refused a create. On a
+		 * WRITABLE mount an absent name would be CREATED, which cannot be
+		 * emulated without clobbering the file being hidden, so the stock
+		 * answer stands -- exactly ghost_create.patch's `err2 &&` reasoning.
+		 *
+		 * Gated on write intent because open(2) is the hottest syscall this
+		 * directory touches and a plain read-only open needs no help: the
+		 * engine answers those through ->permission already. FMODE_CREATED
+		 * means this open just created the file, which can never be a
+		 * pre-existing hidden path.
+		 *
+		 * Ref-walk is guaranteed: complete_walk() ran directly above.
+		 */
+		if (unlikely((open_flag & (O_CREAT | O_TRUNC)) ||
+			     (op->acc_mode & MAY_WRITE)) &&
+		    !(file->f_mode & FMODE_CREATED) &&
+		    ghost_hidden_path(&nd->path)) {
+			if (!(open_flag & O_CREAT))
+				return -ENOENT;
+			error = mnt_want_write(nd->path.mnt);
+			if (error)
+				return error;
+			mnt_drop_write(nd->path.mnt);
+		}
+	}
 	if (!(file->f_mode & FMODE_CREATED))
 		audit_inode(nd->name, nd->path.dentry, 0);
